--- body ---
## Context

Child of tracker 0557. Needed for the acceptance test's edited-collection
and signed-encyclopedia document classes -- neither
tier 1's embedded outline nor tier 2's layout heuristic ever carries who
wrote an entry; no boundary tool does. The SOTA review's finding 6
(`verification/SEGMENTER-FIELD-REVIEW-0028.md`) names ParsCit as citable
proof that plain-text byline detection is feasible without layout at all
(a semi-Markov CRF over capitalization run, punctuation, name-form
dictionaries, sentence position, absence of a verb) -- and notes using it
as a *chapter-boundary confirmation signal* (not just its own
header-extraction task) is a novel application, not an import of a
published one.

## Actions

1. Design a lexical-pattern detector for byline lines: short line,
   capitalization run, name-form tokens, no verb, positioned immediately
   under an accepted heading (from tier 1 or tier 2).
2. Apply it near each accepted entry boundary, not globally.
3. Decide whether byline presence should also *feed back* into boundary
   confidence (an accept-signal, as ticket 0028's 2026-08-31 note
   proposed) or stay a pure enrichment step -- open design question, not
   settled by the ruling.

## Invariants

- Runs on real layout/position data (from tier 1/2's output), not flat
  text -- this should be more reliable than the flat-text ParsCit-style
  sketch the SOTA review only speculated about for seg/1.
- Must not require an author line to accept a boundary -- a thesis (single
  author, no per-chapter byline) is a named acceptance-test document and
  must segment correctly with zero byline signal.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] byline detector implemented and tested against a multi-author
      fixture (edited collection) and a single-author fixture (thesis)
- [ ] the accept-signal-vs-enrichment-only question resolved and recorded
